{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on the capacity question 346de78c (evidence-rules slice\na53e1464, candidate 41215939), 28 October 2026. Rests on the question,\non the slice request and on c20152ef. Rulings ed818781, f73805e4,\nc20152ef and 1bc4f7fd stand.\n\n1. Decision: destination@17 declares no capacity. It keeps legacy\n   admission, as destination@15 and every shipped platform definition\n   do. No kernel extension is authorized for this slice, and the\n   existing-form profile arithmetic stops.\n\n2. Why. The measured problem is the whole-scope opt-in, not the\n   evidence rules. The whole-scope landing (notes/implementation/\n   2026-10-17-capacity-guarantees.md) says: \"No platform default, lane\n   pin or existing cohort opts in through this work\", and the five\n   ceilings are \"temporary deployment ceilings ... logical limits. R4\n   still owes measured ceilings\". The slice request names no capacity\n   mode. Your draft is the first platform definition to opt in, and\n   the opt-in is what turns the compiled holder ledger into an\n   admission term in bytes (scope/src/turn.ts #full; derive\n   reserve.ts fits counts entries alone for a definition without it).\n\n   The baseline, from the shared checkout this evening, by\n   validateDefinition over destinationV15 with and without a forced\n   opt-in (compiled declarations, not a runtime measurement; the\n   ledger is identical in both modes):\n\n   | Holder, destination@15 at main | Entries | Bytes |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | publication | 1,831 | 549,978,112 |\n   | branch | 588 | 154,140,672 |\n   | file-publication | 676 | 177,209,344 |\n\n   Your capture (account-lifecycle-compiled-ledger-3, head 41215939):\n\n   | Holder, destination@17 draft | Entries | Bytes |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | publication | 1,290 | 940,048,384 |\n   | branch | 628 | 459,276,288 |\n   | file-publication | 713 | 524,550,144 |\n   | evidence-request | 91 | 64,487,424 |\n\n   So the Destination at main already compiles to 51 percent of the\n   1,073,741,824-byte ceiling for one publication. The byte term is a\n   property of the static closure rule (contract 17.2, largest of the\n   alternatives times the effects, with every retained value at its\n   ceiling of 262,144 bytes). Adopting whole-scope in any Destination\n   is a design item of its own, after R4's measured ceilings.\n\n3. Why not the outcome-settlement declaration. The failure is static:\n   940,048,384 + 459,276,288 = 1,399,324,672 bytes at the moment the\n   first publication opens, before any entry could settle anything.\n   A declaration that an outcome settles its holder changes when a\n   reservation is released (17.2a, Release), not what the opening\n   entry reserves. It would not admit the first publication. If\n   release-at-outcome is wanted later, it is a contract revision for\n   its own design request under docs/kernel.md section 7, with the\n   whole-scope adoption item.\n\n4. What leaves the draft. Everything added only to fund bytes under\n   the opt-in: the funded self-delivery settlement chain, the\n   sixteen-account profile, the two-slot account type chosen for it,\n   \"all accounts fund before push\", and witness N9. Keep the holder\n   declarations (holds, decisions, bound requests), because they\n   bound operations in entries and the legacy admission counts owed\n   entries (fits: counted plus owed at or under scopeEntries,\n   100,000; settling entries exempt). Keep the design's own counts as\n   guards: 2,048 keys, eight candidates per key, 512 holds, 512\n   generations, 8,192 incidents, three pending slots per key, the\n   before-work refusal of c20152ef point 5. Those are judged by the\n   definition's counts, not by bytes. Where a self-delivery also\n   serves a ruled behaviour, say which and keep that one; the note\n   states what was removed and why.\n\n5. Dimensions, stated plainly. Under legacy admission the runtime\n   admits entries only. Items, records, requests and bytes have no\n   runtime ceiling for destination@17, as for every room deployed\n   today. By the compiled ledger one publication owes 1,290 entries\n   and its branch 628, so the 100,000-entry ceiling admits tens of\n   concurrent publications. The implementation note carries both\n   tables above, labelled as compiled declarations at their heads,\n   with the capture under the evidence directory, and one sentence\n   that destination@17 keeps legacy admission and why.\n\n6. Witnesses. The five simultaneous published-job witness runs under\n   legacy admission. The witnesses of c20152ef point 7 and the\n   remaining Native scenarios stand. No new witness for bytes.\n\n7. Follow-up, the planner's: a backlog item \"Destination adopts\n   whole-scope capacity\" after R4's measured ceilings, starting from\n   the two tables above, with a contract design request if\n   release-at-outcome is part of it. Not this slice.\n\nRetire the question when you have read this. The first-slice promise\nstays active; continue from 41215939 or later.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:346de78c8ca701ae95f8258752ec725a8fe18b0b stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2356b681d86d867af91ed07d208ecccfb97cfaca","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}